      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To assess the reproducibility and validity of the six-minute walk test (6MWT) and predictors of the six-minute walk distance (6MWD) in adolescents with overweight or obesity and intellectual disability (ID)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "Sixty-one adolescents with overweight or obesity and mild-to-moderate ID (intelligent quotient [IQ] 40-70; age 14-22 years) participated in this study. To test reproducibility, 39 of the participants performed the 6MWT twice with an interval of 1 week. To test validity (n\u2009=\u200939), peak oxygen uptake (peak VO(2)) was measured on a bicycle ergometer. For the predictor study (n\u2009=\u200961), weight, height, peak VO(2) and quadriceps strength were measured, and IQ and orthopedic conditions were collected from the participants' medical records."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "There was no significant difference between the means of the two 6MWTs (571.3 vs. 576.5 m; p\u2009=\u20090.452). The intraclass correlation coefficient was 0.82, indicating good reliability. The smallest real difference was 82.6 m. 6MWD correlated significantly with relative peak VO(2) (β\u2009=\u20090.69) indicating validity. Relative peak VO(2), height, IQ, body mass index (BMI) and quadriceps strength are predictors of the 6MWD in this population."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "6MWT is a reliable and valid test in adolescents with overweight or obesity and ID. Low IQ, overweight/obesity and low physical fitness contribute to the outcome of the 6MWT."
        }
      ]},
